 {:seon.task/name "a-runs-request-holds-its-own-run"
  :seon.task/kind :design
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: context, with flow (messages). Found 2026-11-16 reading runtime's 93,941-character reply [M result/dba934d6, b7323904, cf4c836b]: the re-attached session/runtime agent's request holds 56 messages (32k chars) and 43 evals (46k), and 37 of each predate the run that is working now (:seon.agent/started-ms 1794781138029). The agent's pattern pulls every :seon.eval/_agent and :seon.message/_to with no bound by run, so every earlier session's REPL use and every message ever sent to session/<area> is in each new run's request; prompt.md promises the opposite ('a restart of your task begins from its plan'). The reply half is fixed (a reply is what moved since the session joined: seon.server-test/a-reply-carries-what-moved-since-the-sessions-read); this is the request's own content. PRIOR WORK: seon.ctx's pattern and windows (:seon.config.ctx/window, docs/ctx.md#the-pattern), :seon.agent/started-ms, the refine that compacts a record (seon.ctx/roots-context-is-compacted-by-a-refine-that-edits-its-record), a :seon.message read state (flow owns messages; the owner wants reads that mark nothing). DECIDE first, with flow and root: is a run the unit of a conversation (pattern joins only this run's evals and the messages since it began or not yet delivered), or is the session agent's conversation one across attaches and compaction its bound? Then the data model change, never a cut of what is drawn. DECIDED (root, 2026-11-16, the owner's direction): an agent's history is its branch's facts and a request is derived from them, so a new run does not replay earlier runs' evals; context owns it, flow only where it touches the turn loop. READ [both ends]: no fact marks a run's start: seon.agent/begin-run! (agent.clj:2245) writes only the task link, :seon.agent/turn never restarts, and runtime's earlier runs' 42 evals all hang on its turn-0 call (<key>/0), so a turn number cannot separate runs [result/b7323904]. What exists to build on: the ctx pattern already reads a link's transaction (:tx :at on a window, ctx_test's small-window-pattern), seon.ctx.wire/turn-entities reads the calls and evals from the record, and seon.ctx/placed-messages places each message by the call that read it. DESIGN: the run's start is a fact true when written, :seon.agent/run-t (the branch's t when this process began its run, written once by begin-run! at the process's start); the record's eval and model-call joins read their link's :tx; turn-entities keeps the calls and evals linked at or after run-t, and the messages follow the calls that placed them (an undelivered one is placed by the next call as now). Nothing is deleted from the branch: the page and seon_context's whole view still reach the history by the pull's own pattern. COST: the join reads each link's tx it already walks (O(evals + calls) of the record, no new seek); the request shrinks to the run's own (runtime: 6 of 43 evals, 19 of 56 messages). COST: a run-bounded join is the run's evals and messages, O(run) seeks, against O(agent's life) today. BUILT AND REVERTED (context, 2026-11-16, c0ae2e7e8e and its removal): turn-entities kept the calls and placed members linked at or after :seon.agent/started-ms. Wrong in kind: started-ms is a process's start, and root writes it at every spawn AND every wake (system.clj:832), so a parked agent woken lost its own run (seon.agent-test/a-text-reply-stays-in-the-history, a-woken-agent-is-not-sent-its-own-loop went red). A run is not a process. What a run is, decided before any more code: a model agent's run begins when it begins a task (begin-run!, a fresh attach) and continues across parks and wakes and restarts of its process; is a session agent's re-attach a new run or the same one (decisions: 'the same one at every attach')? Root and the owner say which; then the fact is written once where a run begins (begin-run!), and turn-entities reads it. The proof (red first, as written): seon.ctx-test/a-runs-request-holds-only-its-own-runs-evals-and-messages, plus a wake that keeps its run."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/a-runs-request-holds-only-its-own-runs-evals-and-messages}
  :seon.task/estimate {seon.ctx/build {:n 50, :order :linear, :ticks 5000000, :bytes 30000000}}}]
